## Why This Is Asked
This tests deriving a clean DP where subarray cost depends on endpoints, not internal aggregates, and requires careful index handling and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- DP state dp[i][t] for first i elements into t blocks
- Transition over split point p with endpoint cost |A[p+1]-A[i]| 
- Backpointer reconstruction for endpoints
- Time O(n^2 k), space O(nk) (can optimize with rolling arrays)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minEndPointPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][1] = Math.abs(A[0] - A[i]);
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = t - 1; i < n; i++) {
      for (let p = t - 2; p <= i - 1; p++) {
        const val = dp[p][t - 1] + Math.abs(A[p + 1] - A[i]);
        if (val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  let endpoints = [];
  let i = n - 1, t = k;
  while (t > 0) {
    const p = par[i][t];
    endpoints.unshift([p + 1, i]);
    i = p; t--;
  }
  return { cost: dp[n - 1][k], endpoints };
}
```

